{
  "name": "rosetta-y-combinator",
  "snippet": false,
  "code": "use IO.Std.write_line;\n\n// The Y combinator needs to apply a function to itself. In a language where every expression has a\n// type that cannot be written directly: the argument type of `f` would have to contain itself.\n// SELF_APPLY works around it by putting the function in a class, so what gets passed to `apply` is\n// a SELF_APPLY rather than a bare function.\n//\n// The captured function is set when the object is constructed and never changes, and `y` below\n// never calls `y`.\nclass SELF_APPLY[A, B](_f: (SELF_APPLY[A, B]) -> (A -> B)) is\n    apply(r: SELF_APPLY[A, B]) -> (A -> B) => _f(r);\nsi\n\n// `wrap.apply(wrap)` is the self-application. The `(a: A) =>` in front of it matters: without it,\n// working out `f(r.apply(r))` would mean calling `r.apply(r)` again first, and so on forever,\n// because ghūl evaluates an argument before the call it belongs to. Behind the lambda it is only\n// worked out when the returned function is actually applied to something.\ny[A, B](f: (A -> B) -> (A -> B)) -> (A -> B) is\n    let wrap = SELF_APPLY[A, B]((r: SELF_APPLY[A, B]) => (a: A) => f(r.apply(r))(a));\n\n    return wrap.apply(wrap);\nsi\n\nlet factorial = y(recurse => n => if n <= 1 then 1L else cast(n) * recurse(n - 1) fi);\nlet fibonacci = y(recurse => n => if n < 2 then n else recurse(n - 1) + recurse(n - 2) fi);\n\n// `y` is generic, so the same definition also builds functions returning something other than a\n// number.\nlet countdown = y(recurse => n => if n <= 0 then \"liftoff\" else \"{n}, {recurse(n - 1)}\" fi);\n\n// `y` takes a function of one argument, which is less of a restriction than it looks: a function\n// of several is one that takes a tuple, and the literal can destructure it in its parameter list.\nlet gcd = y(recurse => ((a, b): (int, int)) => if b == 0 then a else recurse((b, a % b)) fi);\n\nfor n in 0::10 do\n    write_line(\"factorial({n}) = {factorial(n)}, fibonacci({n}) = {fibonacci(n)}\");\nod\n\nwrite_line(\"countdown(5) = {countdown(5)}\");\nwrite_line(\"gcd(1071, 462) = {gcd((1071, 462))}\");\n\n// ghūl has self-recursive function literals, so this would normally be written\n// `let factorial = n rec => if n <= 1 then 1L else cast(n) * rec(n - 1) fi;` - the combinator here\n// is the exercise, not the way to do it.",
  "fullSource": "use IO.Std.write_line;\n\n// The Y combinator needs to apply a function to itself. In a language where every expression has a\n// type that cannot be written directly: the argument type of `f` would have to contain itself.\n// SELF_APPLY works around it by putting the function in a class, so what gets passed to `apply` is\n// a SELF_APPLY rather than a bare function.\n//\n// The captured function is set when the object is constructed and never changes, and `y` below\n// never calls `y`.\nclass SELF_APPLY[A, B](_f: (SELF_APPLY[A, B]) -> (A -> B)) is\n    apply(r: SELF_APPLY[A, B]) -> (A -> B) => _f(r);\nsi\n\n// `wrap.apply(wrap)` is the self-application. The `(a: A) =>` in front of it matters: without it,\n// working out `f(r.apply(r))` would mean calling `r.apply(r)` again first, and so on forever,\n// because ghūl evaluates an argument before the call it belongs to. Behind the lambda it is only\n// worked out when the returned function is actually applied to something.\ny[A, B](f: (A -> B) -> (A -> B)) -> (A -> B) is\n    let wrap = SELF_APPLY[A, B]((r: SELF_APPLY[A, B]) => (a: A) => f(r.apply(r))(a));\n\n    return wrap.apply(wrap);\nsi\n\nlet factorial = y(recurse => n => if n <= 1 then 1L else cast(n) * recurse(n - 1) fi);\nlet fibonacci = y(recurse => n => if n < 2 then n else recurse(n - 1) + recurse(n - 2) fi);\n\n// `y` is generic, so the same definition also builds functions returning something other than a\n// number.\nlet countdown = y(recurse => n => if n <= 0 then \"liftoff\" else \"{n}, {recurse(n - 1)}\" fi);\n\n// `y` takes a function of one argument, which is less of a restriction than it looks: a function\n// of several is one that takes a tuple, and the literal can destructure it in its parameter list.\nlet gcd = y(recurse => ((a, b): (int, int)) => if b == 0 then a else recurse((b, a % b)) fi);\n\nfor n in 0::10 do\n    write_line(\"factorial({n}) = {factorial(n)}, fibonacci({n}) = {fibonacci(n)}\");\nod\n\nwrite_line(\"countdown(5) = {countdown(5)}\");\nwrite_line(\"gcd(1071, 462) = {gcd((1071, 462))}\");\n\n// ghūl has self-recursive function literals, so this would normally be written\n// `let factorial = n rec => if n <= 1 then 1L else cast(n) * rec(n - 1) fi;` - the combinator here\n// is the exercise, not the way to do it.\n",
